we have a mental health crisis in this  country that there are record levels of  depression anxiety even suicide and  self-harm and one of the huge learnings  from your book was that there was a  surge in depression and anxiety among  young people that hand-in-hand with  widespread smartphone adoption and the  use of social media  there really is a mental health crisis  and I don't think that's an  overstatement at all for adolescents for  young adults we see these really  striking increases in depression in  anxiety in unhappiness loneliness and  those have been either unchanged or  actually improving for a while then  about 2011 or 2012 they start to rise in  these very very steep patterns and it's  not just survey data it's not just  people saying you know I you know I'm  not happy you know I feel useless or or  like I don't enjoy life they're saying  that it also shows up in behavior so  it's not just about self reports  emergency room admissions for self-harm  behaviors have skyrocketed same thing ER  visits for thinking about suicide or  attempting suicide for teenagers have  gone way up and most tragic of all the  actual suicide rate has doubled or even  tripled for young people since 2010 so  you look at all of that data it's so  consistent the the changes are so large  the next question you have to try to  answer is why what happened that might  explain you know why this is going on  and when I first started to see these  trends I had absolutely no idea what the  cost could be it was a real mystery it  was actually misaligned with economic  trends because that period from 2011 to  that the latest data we have is 2018  that was a period of economic expansion  thinks we're doing things were going  fairly  well in in the economy at least in terms  of unemployment and so on  so it actually was misaligned with that  and it was hard to think of any other  event that happened around that time and  kept going in the same direction  especially something that had a big  effect on people's lives but then one  day I stumbled across a poll from the  Pew Center for Research that showed the  end of 2012 was the first time that the  majority of Americans own smartphones  and that's when I started to realize  maybe that might have something to do  with it
 

